<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marco Antonio Bragadin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1523">1523</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marco Antonio Bragadin was the Venetian governor of Famagusta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> during the Ottoman invasion of 1570–1571. He defended the city through a siege of almost a year until supplies ran out and surrendered on terms in August 1571. The Ottoman commander Lala Mustafa Pasha then broke the agreement, and Bragadin was mutilated and flayed alive. His skin was later recovered and is kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marco-antonio-bragadin.jpg" alt="Marco Antonio Bragadin" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiziano_aspetti,_busto_di_marcantonio_bragadin,_1571_ca..JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bragadin was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 21 April 1523. After a short time as a lawyer in 1543 he turned to the navy, held several posts on the Venetian galleys and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fanti</a> da Mar, the marines of the Republic of Venice. Back in Venice he served among the city's magistrates and was made a galley governor in 1560 and again in 1566, though he never actually commanded a ship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1569 he was elected Captain of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and moved to Famagusta, then a rich port, taking the civil governorship of the whole island while a clash with the Ottoman fleet was expected. He strengthened the harbour's defences, among them the Martinengo bastion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> landed on 3 July 1570 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> after a two-month siege, and the severed head of its viceroy, Niccolò Dandolo, was sent to Bragadin.</p><p>The siege of Famagusta began on 17 September 1570. Bragadin led the defence with Lorenzo Tiepolo, Captain of Paphos, and General Astorre Baglioni; Venetian chroniclers put the garrison at about 6,000 against some 100,000 Ottoman troops with 1,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> and about 150 ships blockading the port. The besiegers filled the ground before the walls with earth and dug tunnels to undermine them. In July 1571 they broke into the citadel and were driven back only with heavy losses, and on 1 August, with food and ammunition running out and no relief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, Bragadin asked for terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The terms promised the Westerners safe passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and the evacuation went smoothly for four days. At the surrender ceremony on 5 August the Ottoman commander, Mustafa, accused Bragadin of murdering Turkish prisoners and hiding munitions, cut off his right ear and had his guards cut off the other and his nose, and the Christians still in the city were massacred. After two weeks in prison Bragadin was flayed alive in the main square. In 1580 Girolamo Polidori stole his skin from the arsenal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and it now lies in the Basilica di San Giovanni e Paolo. Historians debate why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> sent no help from Souda in Crete.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marco Antonio Bragadin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nicosia fell in September 1570, and Famagusta surrendered in August 1571 after a long siege; its commander <b>Marco Antonio Bragadin</b> was flayed alive.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-cyprus">Venetian Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Famagusta, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Marco Antonio Bragadin"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5734 6163h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5734 6163h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
